[performance · medium] The "mixed" 100-requirement workload contains only two distinct requirement
texts repeated 50 times each, all with identical zero-valued metadata (nesting_depth 0, empty
citations/parameters) in one flat section with no children, body_text, or content_hash. Repetition
maximizes allocator/regex/engine warm-cache effects on identical input slices, and the flat
structure means hierarchy traversal, dedup-style behavior over heterogeneous content, and
metadata-dependent branches are never exercised — results will not transfer to realistic policies.
Vary text, nesting depth, and section structure in the generated fixture.

      let mut requirements = Vec::with_capacity(100);
-     for i in 0..50 {
-         requirements
-             .push(make_req("Systems must enforce MFA and must require complex passwords", i + 1));
-     }
-     for i in 50..100 {
-         requirements.push(make_req("All systems must enforce MFA", i + 1));
+     for i in 0..100 {
+         let text = match i % 3 {
+             0 => format!("System-{i} must enforce MFA"),
+             1 => format!("Component {i} shall log access events and shall alert admins"),
+             _ => format!("Operators {i} should rotate keys quarterly"),
+         };
+         let mut req = make_req(&text, i + 1);
+         req.nesting_depth = (i % 3) as u8;
+         requirements.push(req);
      }

[maintainability · low] unwrap() runs inside the timed closure (also in
atomize_requirement/atomic_passthrough and atomize_document/100_mixed_requirements). A regression
that makes atomization return Err aborts the entire criterion run with a bare panic message carrying
no bench name, leaving a generic 'called Result::unwrap() on an Err' to debug. Label the failure
with the bench identifier, or validate the input once before the timing loop and keep an unwrapped
fast path inside it.

      c.bench_function("atomize_requirement/compound_2part", |b| {
-         b.iter(|| atomize_requirement(black_box(&req)).unwrap());
+         b.iter(|| {
+             atomize_requirement(black_box(&req))
+                 .expect("atomize_requirement/compound_2part: fixture must atomize")
+         });
      });

[bug · high] Unlike `generate_section_abbreviation`, this helper has neither an empty-result
fallback nor a collision-resolution contract. A section titled purely of punctuation/symbols (e.g.
"!!!") slugs to the empty string, and distinct-but-equivalent titles collapse to the same slug;
either case lets several `OscalGroup`s share one `id` (or carry an empty one), violating OSCAL
identifier uniqueness. Specify a deterministic fallback for empty results and require suffix-based
disambiguation mirroring the abbreviation scheme ("ac" → "ac2" → …) or an explicit error.

  /// - Collapse consecutive hyphens
  /// - Trim leading/trailing hyphens
+ /// - If the result is empty (e.g. title "!!!"), fall back to a deterministic
+ ///   placeholder token; caller MUST then de-duplicate repeated slugs with a
+ ///   numeric suffix ("access-control" → "access-control2"), the same scheme
+ ///   used for section abbreviations, or fail with `ForgeError::CatalogBuild`
  ///
  /// # Examples
- /// - "Access Control Policies" → "access-control-policies"/// - "Data Protection & Privacy" → "data-protection-privacy"
+ /// - "Access Control Policies" → "access-control-policies"
+ /// - "Data Protection & Privacy" → "data-protection-privacy"
  fn generate_group_id(section_title: &str) -> String;

[bug · medium] The 120-char truncation is ambiguous about counting units, and this is a real crash
vector: requirement text comes from untrusted policy documents, so slicing at byte offset 120 inside
a multi-byte UTF-8 codepoint (e.g. an em dash or CJK character spanning offsets 119–122) panics at
runtime. The contract must mandate char-based (`char_indices`/`chars().take(120)`) truncation on
char boundaries; likewise the sentence scan in step 1 should locate '.', '!', '?' without assuming
byte==char positions.

- /// 4. If > 120 chars, truncate to 120 and append "..."
+ /// 4. If longer than 120 Unicode scalar values, truncate at a char boundary
+ ///    using `char_indices()` (never raw byte slicing — mid-codepoint cuts
+ ///    panic) and append "..."
  ///
  /// # Examples
  /// - "Systems shall require MFA. Additional info." → "Systems shall require MFA."
  /// - "All access must be logged" → "All access must be logged"
+ /// - Non-ASCII text must never split a codepoint during truncation
  fn derive_control_title(requirement_text: &str) -> String;

[maintainability · medium] Skipping unmatched citations "with a warning" loses control-to-resource
provenance links with no machine-readable signal — the produced OSCAL simply misses references,
which is hard to detect during later validation and can mask upstream data bugs (typos in citation
IDs, ordering bugs building the map). Change the signature to also surface the skipped IDs so
callers can enforce integrity or fail the run deliberately.

  // /// Citations not found in the resource map are skipped with a warning.
+ // ///
+ // /// Returns the generated links plus the IDs of citations that could not
+ // /// be resolved, so callers can enforce reference integrity explicitly:
  // pub fn generate_control_links(
  //     citations: &[Citation],
  //     resource_map: &HashMap<String, Uuid>,
- // ) -> Vec<OscalLink>;
+ // ) -> GenerateControlLinksOutput;
+ // pub struct GenerateControlLinksOutput {
+ //     pub links: Vec<OscalLink>,
+ //     pub unresolved_citation_ids: Vec<String>,
+ // }

[bug · high] This declaration does not compile: `Uuid::from_bytes` requires a `[u8; 16]` and the
empty array with a comment placeholder fails inference; additionally, `Uuid::new_v5` is not
const-evaluable, so the bytes cannot be 'computed' here — they must be derived offline and pasted as
16 literal bytes. Any byte filled in other than the exact result of
`Uuid::new_v5(&FORGE_NAMESPACE_UUID, b"control-implementation")` silently changes EVERY generated
control-implementation UUID with no detection path, since consumers persist opaque UUIDs. Pin the
literal bytes in the spec itself and require a regression test proving equality with the derivation.

- pub const CONTROL_IMPL_NAMESPACE: Uuid = Uuid::from_bytes([/* computed bytes */]);
+ // Byte-for-byte copy of Uuid::new_v5(&FORGE_NAMESPACE_UUID, b"control-implementation").
+ // Do NOT regenerate; changing these bytes permanently alters all derived UUIDs.
+ pub const CONTROL_IMPL_NAMESPACE: Uuid =
+     Uuid::from_bytes([/* 16 pinned literal bytes */]);
+
+ #[cfg(test)]
+ mod namespace_contract_tests {
+     use super::*;
+
+     #[test]
+     fn control_impl_namespace_matches_new_v5() {
+         assert_eq!(
+             CONTROL_IMPL_NAMESPACE,
+             Uuid::new_v5(&FORGE_NAMESPACE_UUID, b"control-implementation")
+         );
+     }
+ }

[maintainability · medium] The invariant "Component strategy requires a source profile" is enforced
only as a convention in convert.rs, while `build_component_definition` still accepts `Option<&str>`
whose None-case behavior (skip the control-implementations array? emit a default? error?) is
unspecified, and `execute`/`run_component_pipeline` take unvalidated raw `&str`. Every direct caller
of those functions bypasses the CLI-time check. Encode the invariant in types: a `SourceProfile`
newtype constructed via `TryFrom<&str>` (rejecting blank and control-character values), and take
`SourceProfile` (not `Option`) in the component-path builder/pipeline so no code path can reach UUID
seeding without going through validation.

- pub fn build_component_definition(
-     document: &PolicyDocument,
-     source_profile: Option<&str>,
- ) -> Result<ComponentDefinitionEnvelope, ForgeError>;
+ pub struct SourceProfile(String);
+
+ impl TryFrom<&str> for SourceProfile {
+     type Error = ForgeError;
+     fn try_from(raw: &str) -> Result<Self, ForgeError> {
+         let trimmed = raw.trim();
+         if trimmed.is_empty()
+             || trimmed.chars().any(|c| c == '\0' || c.is_control())
+         {
+             return Err(ForgeError::Validation);
+         }
+         Ok(Self(trimmed.to_owned()))
+     }
+ }

[bug · medium] Step 1c implies a single global pass, but each requirement's control ID is generated
independently from its own section abbreviation. Because abbreviations are only guaranteed unique
across *top-level* sections (numeric suffixing happens per section in 1c), two sibling-nested child
sections folded into the same group via step 1d share one abbreviation, and a document whose child
titles differ only by stop words produce identical abbreviation + index combinations once collision
suffixes shift (e.g. sections "Access Control" → AC and "An access Control" → AC both indexed 0 →
both "POL-AC-001"). State explicitly where the global-uniqueness invariant for
`{prefix}-{ABBR}-{NNN}` is enforced, otherwise duplicate control IDs can escape undetected despite
the parenthetical claiming they 'should not happen'.

  ///       c. Resolve abbreviation collisions (numeric suffix: AC → AC2 → AC3)
  ///       d. Recursively collect all requirements (section + children)
  ///       e. For each requirement (indexed 0..N):
  ///          - Validate stable_id is Some
  ///          - Generate control ID via `generate_control_id(abbreviation, index, "POL")`
+ ///          - Ensure the resulting ID is globally unique across the whole
+ ///            document (all suffix-shifting variants share one registry);
+ ///            failure here is a hard error, not silently merged output

[bug · high] Benchmark-validity: this replicated pipeline diverges from the real one. Production
goes through forge::pipeline::prepare_document(), which after extract_citations() additionally runs
parse::annotate_modalities() (WI-33) and parameter::extract_parameters() (WI-34) before
build_catalog/build_component_definition. prepare_document is `pub(crate)` so benches cannot call
it, but re-implementing only part of the stages here means the controls being serialized lack the
`modality` prop and all OSCAL `param` elements (labels/values/constraints) that every real export
contains. For a ~200-requirement fixture that omits a meaningful share of XML payload, so this
benchmark systematically understates the work serialize_catalog_to_xml /
serialize_component_definition_to_xml perform on real artifacts — potentially masking violations of
the <50ms budget. Add the two missing stages (annotated doc must flow into the catalog/component
build; apply to build_component_def_from_fixture identically), or better, make prepare_document
accessible to benches/tests.

      let doc = forge::uuid::assign_stable_ids(atomized);
      let doc = forge::citation::extract_citations(doc).unwrap();
+
+     // Match forge::pipeline::prepare_document() stages 7c/7d:
+     let doc = forge::parse::annotate_modalities(doc).expect("annotate modalities");
+     let mut doc = doc;
+     forge::parameter::extract_parameters(&mut doc).expect("extract parameters");

      let mut trace_links = forge::TraceLinkCollection::new();

[maintainability · medium] Copy-pasted pipeline setup: these ~14 ingest→atomize→assign_ids→cite
stages are duplicated verbatim in build_component_def_from_fixture below, and eight chained unwraps
give no indication of which stage failed if the fixture ever breaks the pipeline. Since
forge::pipeline::prepare_document is pub(crate) and unavailable to benches, extract one local helper
(e.g. fn prepared_document(fixture_path) -> PolicyDocument) consumed by both builders; that removes
the duplication drift risk and lets each step carry an expect() message naming the stage.

- fn build_catalog_from_fixture(fixture_path: &Path) -> forge::oscal::catalog::OscalCatalog {
-     let ingested = forge::ingest::ingest_file(fixture_path, MAX_SIZE_BYTES).unwrap();
+ fn prepared_document(
