[test · medium] Silent no-op bench: if the fixture is missing, tracing::warn! is almost certainly
invisible (criterion harnesses typically install no tracing subscriber) and the early return means
cargo bench exits green having produced zero measurements — a CI regression (renamed/deleted
fixture, wrong cwd) would go unnoticed. FIXTURE_PATH refers to a fixture that is committed alongside
this bench, so treat its absence as a hard error rather than an optional skip.

      if !fixture_path.exists() {
-         tracing::warn!(fixture = %FIXTURE_PATH, "Skipping XML benchmark: fixture not found");
-         return;
+         eprintln!("FATAL: XML benchmark fixture not found: {FIXTURE_PATH}");
+         panic!("required fixture missing: {FIXTURE_PATH}");
      }

[bug · medium] `is_valid` is documented as exactly `errors.is_empty()` but stored as an independent
field, creating two sources of truth: any producer that appends to `errors` after setting the flag
(or any consumer mutating the public fields) can publish `is_valid == true` alongside non-empty
`errors`. Encode the invariant in the type instead of prose — expose it as a method, or route all
construction through a checked constructor/build function so the value cannot go stale.

-     /// Whether the artifact is valid (`errors.is_empty()`).
-     pub is_valid: bool,
+ pub struct ValidationResult {
+     /// Detected or specified model type.
+     pub model_type: OscalModelType,
+     /// All schema validation errors (empty if valid).
+     pub errors: Vec<SchemaError>,
+ }
+
+ impl ValidationResult {
+     /// Derived from `errors`, so it can never disagree with the error list.
+     pub fn is_valid(&self) -> bool {
+         self.errors.is_empty()
+     }
+ }

[maintainability · medium] The exit-code semantics (input/IO = 1, parse = 2, validation/config = 3)
live only in section comments. Since this file declares itself the target contract ('Implementation
must match'), a conforming implementation has no enforced way to reproduce the grouping — the
mapping can silently drift between parser/main modules. The contract should pin it in code by
specifying an `exit_code()` accessor here, so implementations derive codes from one authoritative
place instead of re-transcribing the comments.

- // Contract: ForgeError enum (WI-23 target state)
- // This file defines the target API contract. Implementation must match.
+ impl ForgeError {
+     /// Process exit code per WI-23:
+     /// parse/structure = 2, validation/config = 3, otherwise 1.
+     pub const fn exit_code(&self) -> i32 {
+         match self {
+             Self::NoStructureDetected { .. }
+             | Self::Parse(_)
+             | Self::CatalogBuild(_)
+             | Self::BackMatter(_)
+             | Self::ComponentDefinitionBuild(_) => 2,
+             Self::Validation(_) | Self::Config(_) => 3,
+             _ => 1,
+         }
+     }
+ }

[other · low] Size rendering produces confusing figures at the boundaries of this fixed 1 MiB / 1
KiB split: sizes just under 1 MiB print as "1024.0KB" (which reads as 1 MiB), anything below ~52
bytes prints as "0.0KB", and files ≥ 1 GiB print as large MB values (e.g. "5120.0MB"). Because
`FileTooLarge` also formats `limit_bytes`, a user-supplied small `--max-size` can yield the nonsense
pair "is 0.0KB, exceeding the 0.0KB limit". If the target messages require these exact strings, fine
— otherwise add a byte bucket and a GiB bucket, and decide whether the remaining near-threshold
"1024.0KB" figure is acceptable or whether unit selection should key off the rounded value (or use
unambiguous KiB/MiB/GiB labels).

  fn format_size(bytes: u64) -> String {
-     if bytes < 1_048_576 {
-         format!("{:.1}KB", bytes as f64 / 1024.0)
-     } else {
-         format!("{:.1}MB", bytes as f64 / 1_048_576.0)
+     match bytes {
+         0..=1_023 => format!("{bytes}B"),
+         b if b < 1_073_741_824 => format!("{:.1}KB", b as f64 / 1024.0),
+         b => format!("{:.1}GB", b as f64 / 1_073_741_824.0),
      }
  }

[bug · high] `skip_serializing_if` only affects serialization. Without `#[serde(default)]`,
deserializing any previously generated catalog (or external OSCAL document) whose groups lack
`props`/`links` keys fails with `missing field`, breaking round-trips. Note the live implementation
in `src/oscal/catalog.rs` correctly uses `#[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]`,
so this contract as written contradicts the shipping struct — fix the contract. (`OscalProp.ns` is
safe as-is because serde defaults missing `Option<T>` fields to `None`.)

  //   pub struct OscalGroup {
  //       pub id: String,
  //       pub title: String,
- //       #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
+ //       #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
  //       pub props: Vec<OscalProp>,
- //       #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
+ //       #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
  //       pub links: Vec<OscalLink>,
+ //       #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = "Vec::is_empty")]
+ //       pub controls: Vec<OscalControl>,
+ //   }

[documentation · medium] This doctest cannot compile as written: `build_catalog` and `doc` are
undefined, and doctests compile against the crate as an external dependency, so items of the
defining module (including `serialize_to_yaml` itself) are NOT automatically in scope — an explicit
`use` with a full path is required. (Bare `?` at top level is fine on modern rustdoc, which
auto-wraps into `fn main() -> Result<(), _>`, but the unresolved identifiers still guarantee a
`cargo test --doc` failure once this contract is implemented verbatim in src/export/yaml.rs.) Mark
the block `ignore`/`text`, or add hidden (`#`) setup lines with imports and a stubbed input.

+ /// # Examples
+ /// ```no_run
+ /// # use crate::export::yaml::serialize_to_yaml;
+ /// # use crate::error::ForgeError;
+ /// # let doc = /* load OSCAL doc */ todo!();
+ /// # fn build_catalog(_d: &Doc, _o: Option<&Path>) -> Result<Catalog, ForgeError> { todo!() }
+ /// # fn main() -> Result<(), ForgeError> {
  /// let catalog = build_catalog(&doc, None)?;
  /// let yaml = serialize_to_yaml(&catalog)?;
  /// assert!(yaml.starts_with("catalog:") || yaml.contains("catalog:"));
+ /// # Ok(())
+ /// # }
+ /// ```

